  "character_bible": "The recurring fictional chalk-line figure is an anonymous neutral adult with a round unfeatured head, slim proportions, unchanged head silhouette, consistent line weight, simple off-white chalk-line details, and black charcoal clothing blocks. Facial detail is absent except for restrained dot eyes when quiet shame, hesitation, or self-consciousness must be visible; never use realistic features, expressive cartoon exaggeration, identifiable age, gender, ethnicity, celebrity resemblance, or a redesigned mascot. Keep the figure's proportions, posture language, and charcoal/off-white treatment identical across scenes. The body communicates the psychology: squared shoulders and locked elbows indicate forced self-reliance; a hovering hand indicates an unspoken request; a clenched free hand indicates protection of the identity 'I can do it alone'; a slight lean toward the handrail indicates an emerging willingness to use support. The figure may appear alone or as a barely differentiated second chalk-line presence, but never introduce a named protagonist or a visually dominant second character. When support is accepted, preserve dignity and neutrality: the figure remains upright, not rescued or infantilized.",
  "environment_bible": "The primary environment is a symbolic corridor rendered as a continuous black-ink architectural space on an off-white paper field. Its handrail is the recurring visual anchor: one controlled warm-gold #FFD700 line against black and off-white surroundings, with no other saturated color. The corridor may subtly bend, narrow, lengthen, lose symmetry, or become difficult to read as the figure's internal interpretation changes. Floors should contain sparse unstable ink strokes, slight perspective distortions, and interrupted edges rather than literal hazards. Charcoal darkness gathers at thresholds, corners, and distant vanishing points to suggest isolation and uncertain control. Use empty wall space and deep negative space for psychological pressure. Support can be represented by the same handrail, a stable edge, or a quiet off-screen presence, but avoid literal rescue scenes, hospitals, offices, desks, phones, notebooks, calendars, crowds, or explanatory props. If a second figure appears, keep it peripheral, simplified, and nonverbal. All surfaces remain text-free and free of signs or markings. Reuse the same corridor geometry, handrail height, gold hue, paper texture, and ink density throughout the video so visual changes reflect psychological interpretation rather than a new world.",
  "opening_visual_contract": {
    "thumbnail_scene": "A recurring fictional chalk-line figure stands on a subtly bending black-ink corridor, gripping a single warm-gold handrail with one hand while the other hand hovers before asking for help. The figure keeps a composed posture, but the corridor narrows toward darkness and the unsupported hand exposes the contradiction between needing support and refusing to request it. This is the thumbnail's core visual promise: support is physically available but psychologically difficult to use.",
    "first_frame_scene": "Open on an extreme close-up of the same warm-gold handrail cutting diagonally through a nearly black corridor. The recurring figure is not yet fully visible; only one charcoal sleeve and a tense chalk-line hand enter the frame, gripping the rail. The gold line is the sole accent, surrounded by rough off-white ink texture and deep negative space. Do not reproduce the thumbnail composition literally; begin inside the symbol, with the handrail appearing first as an ambiguous object of tension rather than an obvious source of help. No text or readable markings.",
    "first_15s_visuals": [
      "0-3 seconds: Hold on the close-up of the gold handrail and the gripping hand. The fingers tighten slightly while the corridor's black ink edges tremble almost imperceptibly, suggesting effort without showing an external threat.",
      "3-6 seconds: The camera slowly pulls back to reveal the recurring figure standing upright on an uneven corridor floor. The other hand is visible at the side, closed and motionless, while the figure's round unfeatured head remains turned toward the distant darkness.",
      "6-9 seconds: A faint off-screen chalk-line hand enters near the frame edge, not touching the figure. The recurring figure notices it, shifts weight toward the rail, then straightens rigidly and withdraws the free hand, making the refusal visible without dialogue or text.",
      "9-12 seconds: The corridor briefly splits into two ink-perspective paths: one path continues alone into darkness, while the other follows the gold handrail toward a calmer off-white opening. Keep both paths within one continuous environment, with no hard panel division or collage.",
      "12-15 seconds: The figure remains between the paths, still holding the handrail. The gold line steadies the floor perspective for one moment, then the image settles on the figure's unresolved posture: supported physically, but not yet willing to interpret support as strength."
    ]
  }
}
